“In their intercourse they are not remiss, and are rewarded by the Son of Heaven. Joyous are the princely men; the Son of Heaven gives them commands. Joyous are the princely men; blessings and emoluments are renewed to them.”
“Richly carved is that jade libation ladle, with yellow aromatic wine inside it. Easy and self-possessed is the princely man, on whom blessings and emoluments descend. The hawk flies up to heaven; the fishes leap in the deep. Easy and self-possessed is the princely man—does he not exert an influence upon men?”
“Ah, you ministers and officers, be reverent in your public duties! The king has given you perfect rules; come and consult, come and deliberate.”

Spring and Autumn Annals (Chunqiu)
“In the third month, the Earl of Zheng sent Yuan to come and restore Beng” [8th year of Duke Yin];
“In summer, in the sixth month, the Duke met with the Marquis of Qi, the Duke of Song, the Marquis of Chen, and the Earl of Zheng, and together they made a covenant at You” [27th year of Duke Zhuang]; “In the sixth month, the Duke met with the Duke of Song, the Marquis of Chen, the Marquis of Wei, the Earl of Zheng, the Baron of Xu, the Earl of Cao, and Zhao Dun of Jin; on the guiyou day, together they made a covenant at Xincheng” [14th year of Duke Wen];
“In winter, in the tenth month, the Duke met with the Marquis of Jin, the Duke of Song, the Marquis of Wei, the Earl of Zheng, the Earl of Cao, the Viscount of Ju, the Viscount of Zhu, the Viscount of Teng, the Earl of Xue, the Earl of Qi, and the Viscount of Lesser Zhu, and together they laid siege to Qi” [18th year of Duke Xiang]; “The Duke arrived from invading Qi” [19th year of Duke Xiang];
“The Duke of Song, the Marquis of Chen, the Marquis of Wei, and the Earl of Cao met the army of Jin at Feilin and invaded Zheng” [1st year of Duke Xuan]; “The Duke met the army of Jin at Wa” [8th year of Duke Ding].

