Third Session

Five Policy Questions

Question: Among the sacrificial canons, none is greater than the suburban sacrifice (jiao); since antiquity, emperors and kings have all accorded it the highest importance, yet the teachings of ritual specialists regarding it are starkly different. Those who advocate joint sacrifice base themselves on the passage in the Book of Yu (Yu shu) on performing the lei sacrifice to the Supreme Lord; those who advocate separate sacrifices follow the system of the Director of Music (Siyue) in the Rites of Zhou (Zhouli). Which view is correct? From the Han dynasty onward, debates over separate versus joint sacrifice have been profuse and discordant; in actual practice, which of the two was more commonly adopted? As for the rite of honoring ancestors by associating them in the sacrifice to Heaven: some have elevated the primordial ancestor, some have elevated the sovereign who received the Mandate, some have matched different ancestors separately with Heaven and the Five Emperors, and some have associated both the dynastic founder and subsequent ancestors together. By what standard should one adjudicate among these? Let us reverently reflect upon how Our Grand Progenitor, Emperor Gao (Taizu Gaohuangdi), when he first pacified the realm, immediately established…

