Third Session

Five Policy Questions

Question: Among the sacrificial canons, none is greater than the suburban sacrifices (jiao). Since antiquity, emperors and kings have all held them in high esteem, yet the interpretations of ritualists differ sharply. Those who advocate joint sacrifice base themselves on the text regarding the "sacrifice to the Supreme Lord" (silui) in the Book of Yu (Yushu); those who advocate separate sacrifices follow the system of the Director of Music (Siyue) in the Rites of Zhou (Zhouli). Which view is correct? From the Han dynasty onward, debates over separate versus joint sacrifice have been profuse and varied; which practice was more frequently adopted and followed? In the rites of honoring ancestors as coadjutors in the sacrifice to Heaven, some enshrined the founding ancestor, some enshrined the sovereign who received the Mandate, some paired individual emperors with specific celestial deities, and some paired both the founding ancestor and later emperors together. How should one strike a proper balance among these? Reflecting upon our Grand Progenitor, Emperor Gao (Taizu Gao Huangdi), when he first pacified the realm, he immediately established…
